  "Question": "What is the reciprocal of \\(\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} + \\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{1}{6}\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\dfrac{2}{5}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac{6}{5}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac{5}{2}\\)",
    "E": "\\(5\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "First evaluate the number inside the parentheses by using a common denominator:\n\\[\n\\frac12+\\frac13=\\frac36+\\frac26=\\frac56.\n\\]\nA reciprocal multiplies the original number to give \\(1\\). Since \\(\\frac56\\ne0\\), its reciprocal is \\(\\frac65\\), as\n\\[\n\\frac56\\cdot\\frac65=1.\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac65}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
